# losos-security -- the operating-system half of the security report.
#
# fwupd answers "is the firmware trustworthy". This answers "is the operating
# system set up the way a careful person would set it up" -- TPM, disk
# encryption, kernel lockdown, module signing, IOMMU, the hardening sysctls --
# and serves the answer on the system bus in fwupd's own attribute shape, so
# GNOME's Privacy & Security panel lists both in one report rather than in two
# applications. The panel change that consumes it is
# recipes/30-gnome/gnome-control-center/files/patches/0002-*.
#
# The sources are this repository's own, so there is no dl_urls here and
# nothing to pin -- with one exception, below.
#
# THIS MEMBER MAKES THE WHOLE LAYER'S JAIL SHARE THE HOST NETWORK. pm derives
# the network capability from `cargo` and applies it per BUILD FILE, not per
# step (C8), so every member of losos-05-core builds with the host's network
# namespace because this one needs crates.io. That is the cost of the first
# Rust package in the tree and it is paid by its neighbours, which is why it is
# written here rather than discovered later.
#
# What is NOT lost is the pin. `--locked` refuses to move Cargo.lock, and that
# file records a SHA-256 for every crate, checked by cargo on extraction -- the
# same guarantee sources.lock gives for a tarball, enforced by a different
# tool. An unpinned `cargo build` would be the problem; this is not one.
name: losos-security
version:
- '0'
- '1'
- '0'
dependencies: []
steps:

# Copied out of the recipe directory before building, because that directory is
# mounted read-only (C7) and cargo writes target/ next to the manifest unless
# told otherwise. Copying is cheaper to reason about than keeping every cargo
# output path pointed elsewhere.
- stage: Build
  name: stage-sources
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - mkdir -p /build/src/losos-security
  - cp -a @RECIPE@/files/losos-security/. /build/src/losos-security

# --target, not the host default: everything above the toolchain layer is musl,
# and a cargo build with no --target silently produces a binary linked against
# the build host's libc that will not run on the image.
#
# --config build.rustflags rather than RUSTFLAGS, because a step cannot set an
# environment variable and `env` is banned (see CLAUDE.md). The flag list comes
# from manifest/toolchain.yaml like every other flag in the tree; a recipe that
# spelled its own -Clto would be outside the scheme with nothing saying so.
- stage: Build
  name: compile
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - cargo build --release --locked --manifest-path /build/src/losos-security/Cargo.toml --target-dir /build/b/losos-security --target @ARCH_RUST_TARGET@ --config build.rustflags=@RUSTFLAGS_CARGO@

# The suite runs here rather than nowhere. It is ten tests over a fixture
# directory tree -- no TPM, no root, no network -- and it is the only thing that
# distinguishes "the checks compile" from "the checks say the right thing".
- stage: Build
  name: test
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - cargo test --release --locked --manifest-path /build/src/losos-security/Cargo.toml --target-dir /build/b/losos-security --target @ARCH_RUST_TARGET@ --config build.rustflags=@RUSTFLAGS_CARGO@

- stage: Build
  name: install
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - install -D -m 0755 /build/b/losos-security/@ARCH_RUST_TARGET@/release/losos-security /dest/usr/bin/losos-security

- stage: Test
  name: staged
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - test -x /dest/usr/bin/losos-security
